WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.765
Use-after-free prevention is built from consistent habits.

2
00:00:03.765 --> 00:00:05.857
Make ownership and lifetime explicit.

3
00:00:05.857 --> 00:00:10.040
Prefer values and standard containers when they express the design.

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:18.407
Use std::unique_ptr for exclusive ownership, std::shared_ptr only for intentional shared lifetime, and std::weak_ptr for observation when shared

5
00:00:18.407 --> 00:00:19.662
ownership already exists.

6
00:00:19.662 --> 00:00:21.754
Keep borrowed access short-lived.

7
00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:30.121
Treat raw pointers, references, iterators, spans, string views, and callbacks as non-owning access unless the API clearly says otherwise.

8
00:00:30.121 --> 00:00:35.559
Review any stored borrow, returned reference, captured pointer, or view into mutable data.

9
00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:38.487
Avoid raw owning pointers in new designs.

10
00:00:38.487 --> 00:00:41.834
Pay special attention to invalidation and delayed execution.

11
00:00:41.834 --> 00:00:43.926
Container mutations can stale access.

12
00:00:43.926 --> 00:00:47.273
Reset and release operations can change ownership quickly.

13
00:00:47.273 --> 00:00:51.874
Callbacks and async work may run after the originating scope ends.

14
00:00:51.874 --> 00:00:55.221
Legacy boundaries need clear allocation and cleanup contracts.

15
00:00:55.221 --> 00:01:01.496
When a lifetime rule feels hard to explain, improve the design before relying on memory.

16
00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:09.445
Rename the role, change the type, shorten the borrow, or add a wrapper that makes the intended ownership obvious.

17
00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:11.955
Finally, use tooling and review together.

18
00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:20.322
Sanitizers, static analysis, focused tests, and CI builds can expose defects, but they work best when ownership design is clear.

19
00:01:20.322 --> 00:01:25.760
The durable question is simple: can any access outlive the object it uses?
